.TH REV 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
rev \- reverse the characters of each line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rev
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rev
copies each
.I file
to standard output, with the characters of every line in reverse order.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still read.
.PP
A line ends at an LF, and a lone CR is an ordinary character.
The line ending is not reversed: the LF stays at the end of its line,
and so does one CR directly before it.
The last line of a file may have no LF.
It is written without one, so it runs into the first line of the next
file, and a CR that ends it stays in place too.
.SS Characters
In UTF-8 mode, described under
.BR ENVIRONMENT ,
a line is decoded as UTF-8, reversed one code point at a time, and
encoded again.
Combining characters are not treated specially, so a combining mark that
followed its base character comes before it in the output, where it
combines with the character that precedes it.
A line that contains a sequence the decoder rejects is written unchanged:
a byte from 0x80 to 0xBF that does not continue a character, a byte from
0xC0 to 0xFF without enough such bytes after it, or an overlong form of
an ASCII character.
Other invalid sequences, such as encoded surrogates and values above
U+10FFFF, are reversed as single characters, and an overlong form of
any other character is rewritten in its shortest form.
.PP
Outside UTF-8 mode, each byte is a character.
The bytes of a multibyte UTF-8 character are then reversed too, and the
output is not valid UTF-8.
.SH OPTIONS
.B rev
has no options other than
.BR \-\-help .
An argument that starts with a dash is an option wherever it appears,
even after the
.I file
operands, except
.BR \- ,
which is standard input.
.B \-\-
ends the options, so a file whose name starts with a dash follows it or
is given with a leading
.BR ./ .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
Any other option prints an error and the usage message on standard error
and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that a later argument such as
.B \-\-help
is a file name.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set decides whether UTF-8 mode is on.
When its value is exactly
.BR C ,
UTF-8 mode is off.
Otherwise UTF-8 mode is on when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, which it
is on Windows 10 version 1903 and later, and off when it is not.
The value
.B C
also turns off the conversion of console output described in
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B rev
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, a
.I file
could not be opened (a directory is reported as
.BR "Is a directory" ),
memory ran out, or standard output could not be written when
.B rev
flushed it at exit.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before
.B rev
finished writing to it.
Output to a pipe is buffered, so when the closed pipe is first noticed
by the final flush, as when all the output fits in the buffer,
.B rev
reports an error and exits with status 1 instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Reverse a line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo hello | rev
olleh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reverse each line of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rev notes.txt >notes.rev
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the last dot-separated field of a name, by reversing it, cutting
the first field, and reversing that:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo archive.tar.gz | rev | cut \-d. \-f1 | rev
gz
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mirror a file, reversing both the order of its lines and the characters
in each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tac notes.txt | rev
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Files and standard streams are binary, with no CRLF translation, so a
file with CRLF line endings is read and written as it is.
Keeping the CR with the LF is specific to Windows: on Unix, busybox
reverses that CR with the rest of the line.
Output to a console is converted to the console's code page, as
described in
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
A NUL byte ends the text of its line: the NUL and the rest of the line,
including its line ending, are discarded.
The text before the NUL is reversed and written with no line ending, so
it runs into the next line.
This holds in and out of UTF-8 mode.
.PP
A read error ends a file as if it had reached its end, with no message.
.PP
A last line with no LF is lost, with no message, when its length in
bytes is 255, 351, 459, 580, 716, or a later length at which
.B rev
enlarges its line buffer, unless an earlier line, in this or a previous
file, was at least as long.
.PP
Each line is held in memory whole, and in UTF-8 mode also as a decoded
copy of four bytes per byte.
The length of a line is kept in an
.BR int ,
so a line of 2 GiB or more is not supported.
.PP
Options of other implementations, such as the
.B \-0
of util-linux, are errors.
.PP
File names may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903
and later, may use backslashes, and may start with a drive letter.
The device names that busybox accepts, such as
.IR /dev/null ,
work as operands; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dos2unix (1),
.BR iconv (1),
.BR tac (1)
